<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Banten</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Banten is a province at the western tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, separated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-java">West Java</a> in 2000. Its old port of Banten, on the bay of the same name, was the centre of the Banten Sultanate, founded after Muslim forces from Demak and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirebon">Cirebon</a> took it from the Sunda Kingdom in the 1520s, and became one of Asia's great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> ports. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> gained control of it in the late 17th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/banten.jpg" alt="Banten" width="320" height="147" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banten_in_Indonesia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Banten lies at the western tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. It is bordered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java-sea">Java Sea</a> on the north, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda Strait</a> on the west and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> on the south, and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-java">West Java</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a> on the east. The Sunda Strait carries sea lanes linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, and Banten forms a link between Java and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>. The province covers 9,355.76 square kilometres and ranges from sea level to 2,000 metres, with lowlands in the north and south and hills in the centre.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the fourth and fifth centuries the area belonged to the kingdom of Tarumanagara. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> inscription found in 1947 in Munjul describes life under Purnawarman. After Tarumanagara fell to an attack by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> became part of the Sunda Kingdom. Islam began to spread in the 15th century and by the late 16th century had replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> as the main religion, with the establishment of the Banten Sultanate. According to the Babad Banten, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunan-gunung-jati">Sunan Gunung Jati</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maulana-hasanuddin">Maulana Hasanuddin</a> spread Islam widely.</p><p>The Portuguese were established in Banten before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, and the English, French and Danish also traded there. The Portuguese left in 1601 after the Dutch destroyed their fleet off the coast. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> gradually weakened the sultanate, and on 22 November 1808 Governor-General Herman Willem Daendels declared it absorbed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>. Japan occupied the region from March 1942 and built the Saketi-Bayah railway to carry brown <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>, using about 1,000 forced labourers, rōmusha, drawn mainly from Banten; casualties are estimated at 20,000 to 60,000, not counting mine workers. Banten became a separate province on 17 October 2000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The capital of the province is Serang and its largest city is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangerang">Tangerang</a>. Most residents are Muslims, and the Bantenese, at 47 per cent, are the largest ethnic group. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baduy">Baduy</a> people of Kanekes practise Sunda Wiwitan and keep their ancestral customs. The Great Mosque of Banten and Ujung Kulon National Park, a sanctuary of the Javan rhinoceros, are among its notable sites.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Banten” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Chinese official Zhao Rugu noted around 1225 that Sunda produced pepper of high quality, and Tomé Pires, writing about 1512–1515, listed its ports, among them <b>Banten</b>, Pontang, Tangerang, Kalapa and Cimanuk, which shipped pepper, rice and other goods to Malacca and beyond.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sunda-kingdom">Sunda Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1527 forces from Demak and Cirebon, led by Sunan Gunung Jati and his son Maulana Hasanuddin, took the port of <b>Banten</b> from the Sunda Kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/banten-sultanate">Banten Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1343 Majapahit troops under Gajah Mada, joined according to Balinese tradition by Arya Damar, defeated the last king, remembered as Sri Astasura Ratna Bumi <b>Banten</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/bedulu-kingdom">Bedulu Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Expelled from <b>Banten</b> in 1682, the English East India Company sought a new source of pepper and in 1685 founded a post at Bencoolen (Bengkulu) by agreement with local chiefs.</blockquote><a href="/state/bencoolen">Bencoolen</a></li><li><blockquote>Sunan Gunung Jati, one of the Wali Songo, allied with Demak and helped take <b>Banten</b> and Sunda Kalapa from Sunda.</blockquote><a href="/state/cirebon-sultanate">Cirebon Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Sultan Ageng Tirtayasa of <b>Banten</b> divided Cirebon among them, making the elder, Pangeran Martawijaya, Sultan Sepuh, the senior sultan, of Kasepuhan.</blockquote><a href="/state/kasepuhan-sultanate">Kasepuhan Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
